that ran in them, are written as one immutable batch\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m   \r\u001b[6C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m8\u001b[11G\u001b[38;2;117;113;94m// file and the catalogue replaced to name it as the head. So a crash loses what ran since the last\r\u001b[6C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m8\u001b[11G\u001b[38;2;117;113;94m// flush and nothing before it, and a clean stop, which flushes, loses nothing. Every\r\u001b[6C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m8\u001b[11G\u001b[38;2;117;113;94m// Cadence::snapshotSteps the world's save is written beside them, the last two kept with every batch\r\u001b[6C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m8\u001b[11G\u001b[38;2;117;113;94m// since the older, and what is older than that removed. Every file is followed by its 64-bit\r\u001b[6C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m8\u001b[11G\u001b[38;2;117;113;94m// FNV-1a, which is also a batch's identity in the chain.\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[49m\u001b[39m\u001b[2m 86 \u001b[22m \u001b[38;2;102;217;239mclass\u001b[39m \u001b[38;2;166;226;46mJournal\u001b[39m                       \u001b[76G\u001b[K\u001b[27;1H\u001b[24;3H\u001b[?25h"]
